# Signal gem for ORuby

Ruby's `Signal` module (`trap`, `list`, `signame`), `SignalException` and
`Interrupt`, as MRI 4.1 has them (ruby/spec core/signal and core/exception pass,
except the examples that need Ruby threads). The process gem loads it.

## Install

```go
import (
	"github.com/oruby/oruby"

	_ "github.com/oruby/oruby/gem/signal"
)
```

## Docs

Signal gem follows ORuby "core gems code" to be as close as possible to MRI Ruby core,
so you can see consult docs for Ruby Signal module.

Implementation is thin scripting over standard Go signal handling. Which means that
everything about Go signal handling should be considered. Signal constants are added
for Windows, Linux and macOS, plus SIGINT and SIGKILL which are supported on all Go platforms.

## Implementation

Go receives OS signals on a goroutine (`signal.Notify`). That goroutine never
runs Ruby: it marks the signal pending and arms a one-shot mruby
`code_fetch_hook` (`go-signal.c`, `signal.go` in the root package). The VM calls
the hook before its next instruction, on the goroutine that runs Ruby, and the
hook calls `Signal.__oruby_signal__(signo)`, which runs the trap handler or
raises `SignalException` / `Interrupt` right there, like any raise. No API call
is made from the signal goroutine, so no locking is needed.

A state that is not running Ruby when a signal arrives handles it at its next
instruction. Go code that waits can handle it earlier: select on
`mrb.SignalWake()` and call `mrb.CheckSignals()`, raising what it returns
(`sleep` does that; `Process.kill` to the own process does it before returning,
as MRI does). A second default-handled signal (SIGINT, SIGTERM, ...) arriving
while the first still waits for Ruby ends the process the OS way, so Ctrl-C
twice works even when a Go call blocks.

`signal.InstallDefaults(mrb)` installs MRI's default handlers (SIGINT raises
`Interrupt`, SIGHUP/QUIT/TERM/ALRM/USR1/USR2 raise `SignalException`); the
`oruby` command calls it. Without it, untrapped signals keep the OS default.
Signals are process-wide: the state that traps a signal last receives it.

## Usage

```go
package main

import (
	"github.com/oruby/oruby"
	_ "github.com/oruby/oruby/gem/signal"
)

func main() {
	mrb := oruby.MrbOpen()
	defer mrb.Close()

	// Goroutine which sends SIGUSR1 to current process 1 second after execution
	go func() {
		<-time.After(1 * time.Second)
		_= syscall.Kill(syscall.Getpid(), syscall.SIGUSR1)
	}()

	// ORuby script with infinite loop that gets interupted via signal
	_,_= mrb.Eval(`
		$testv = 0
		Signal.trap("USR1") { p "Received USR1"; raise StopIteration }
		Signal.trap(0) { p "Exited." }

		# Infinite loop
		loop do
			$testv = 1
		end
	`)

	println("Out of the Eval")
}

// Should output:
// "Received USR1"
// "Out of the Eval"
// "Exited."
```

